{"`-ms-align-items` is not a valid CSS property. The correct IE10 prefixed property is `-ms-flex-align`, which is already declared on the previous line. This line has no effect and should be removed.": 0.6452847123146057, "`-webkit-box-align` and `-ms-flex-align` do not accept the same values as `align-items`. Values like `flex-start`/`flex-end` need to be mapped to `start`/`end` for these legacy prefixed properties. Without this mapping, the prefixes are ineffective for the very browsers they target.": 0.5998228192329407, "`-webkit-box-ordinal-group` is 1-based, while the standard `order` property is 0-based. Passing the same `$val` to both means old-WebKit ordering will be off by one. Typically this is handled with `-webkit-box-ordinal-group: $val + 1`.": 0.8565528988838196, "This replaces standard CSS with vendor-prefix mixins, moving in the opposite direction of modern best practices. The native `display: inline-flex` and `align-items: baseline` properties are universally supported without prefixes. The mixins generate ~10 extra lines of unnecessary vendor-prefixed CSS output, adding bloat rather than optimizing performance as the PR title claims.": 0.22994311153888702, "Adding `flexbox()` to `.small-action` silently disables `float: left` / `float: right` on its children (`.topic-avatar`, `.avatar`, `button`). Per the CSS spec, `float` has no effect on flex items. If the floats were intentional for positioning, the layout will shift; if they're now redundant, they should be removed to avoid confusion.": 0.705876886844635, "Inconsistent formatting: this mixin uses 4-space indentation (rest of file uses 2-space), and `align-items:$alignment` is missing the space after the colon.": 0.11720525473356247}